  "tour": {
    "title": "Pentecost as a Reverse Babel?",
    "intro": "This tour starts with Babel’s scattering and then listens for what Acts 2 is doing with the nations, languages, and the Spirit. It also leaves room for an important qualification: the “reverse Babel” idea is not merely that everyone becomes culturally identical, but that God’s good news is heard across real linguistic and cultural difference and creates a new kind of community.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "recordId": "naked-bible:podcast:225",
        "startSec": 95,
        "endSec": 395,
        "why": "Begin with the theological backstory. The speaker connects Acts 2 to “the division of the nations in Genesis 10 and 11,” then explains Babel and Deuteronomy 32 as the backdrop for the nations’ estrangement and says that Pentecost inaugurates “the reversal of that, this whole situation, reclaiming the nations.”",
        "episodeTitle": "Naked Bible 37: Acts 2:1-21",
        "sourceId": "naked-bible",
        "source": "Naked Bible Podcast",
        "audioUrl": "https://nakedbiblepodcast.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/02/NakedBible37_Acts2_1_21.mp3",
        "durationSec": 3857,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "forty-minutes-ot:podcast:6e2330f5bb45617d7406fb920c40990e",
        "startSec": 1870,
        "endSec": 2165,
        "why": "This is the clearest direct comparison of the two scenes. The speaker contrasts Babel’s people gathered “for an evil purpose” to “make a name for themselves” with Pentecost, where God comes down, gives the Spirit, and people hear the gospel in a unified way. It also adds the Pentecost/Sinai connection: the festival associated with receiving the law becomes a proclamation of its fulfillment in Christ.",
        "episodeTitle": "40 Minutes In The Old Testament: Episode 16",
        "sourceId": "forty-minutes-ot",
        "source": "40 Minutes in the Old Testament",
        "audioUrl": "https://adbarker.com/stream/tfVE3XBxC9JCmb97VsZt7vpT/traffic.libsyn.com/secure/40minot/40_min_16_final_2.mp3?dest-id=245197",
        "durationSec": 2435,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "spoken-gospel:podcast:76ae8290-d131-11f0-a488-778b4582e38d",
        "startSec": 2180,
        "endSec": 2445,
        "why": "Now hear the Acts 2 details in their own narrative setting: people from many nations hear “the great works that God has done through Jesus” in their own languages. The speakers frame Babel as a prideful attempt to live without dependence on God, and Pentecost as the beginning of Abraham’s promised blessing reaching the scattered nations.",
        "episodeTitle": "Acts Overview: The Day of Pentecost",
        "sourceId": "spoken-gospel",
        "source": "Spoken Gospel",
        "audioUrl": "https://traffic.megaphone.fm/SPG2526509417.mp3",
        "durationSec": 4525,
        "adInsertionDrift": true
      },
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:church-city",
        "startSec": 2280,
        "endSec": 2580,
        "why": "This clip keeps the “reverse Babel” reading from becoming a simplistic story about uniformity. The speakers emphasize that Pentecost gathers Israelites from “all the nations” and that this multicultural renewal produces a community with new economic relationships, care for widows, and a way of resolving cultural conflict—the “opposite of Babylon” embodied in social life.",
        "episodeTitle": "The City E15: Is the Church a City?",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee/episodes/5fce19b6-ba2e-443f-8e22-82973e92a7e8/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee&awEpisodeId=5fce19b6-ba2e-443f-8e22-82973e92a7e8",
        "durationSec": 3247,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "spoken-gospel:podcast:6eef2130-b99a-44e4-8393-8deae06cc493",
        "startSec": 1015,
        "endSec": 1140,
        "why": "End with the broad biblical-theological payoff. The speakers summarize Acts as turning “the scattered nations from a curse into a blessing,” and connect Pentecost’s languages, visions, and worldwide mission with the kingdom of Jesus replacing the old empire—not by human self-rule, but through dependence on God.",
        "episodeTitle": "Daniel Introduction",
        "sourceId": "spoken-gospel",
        "source": "Spoken Gospel",
        "audioUrl": "https://traffic.megaphone.fm/SPG2538539015.mp3?updated=1664486072",
        "durationSec": 2537,
        "adInsertionDrift": true
      }
    ],
    "closing": "Taken together, these clips suggest that Pentecost is a reversal of Babel in direction and purpose: Babel gathers humanity around self-made greatness and ends in confusion and scattering; Pentecost gathers the nations around God’s mighty works, gives understanding across languages, and sends people back out as a blessing. The reversal is therefore not the erasure of difference, but the creation of faithful communion and shared mission under Jesus.",
    "totalSeconds": 1285
  },
